Globus Sensation (Globus Pharyngeus)
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Sensation of a lump in the throat, Relief with eating/drinking, Complete absence of true dysphagia, Normal nasendoscopy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-021
Reason for Visit: Acute distress and severe panic regarding an impending airway obstruction and inability to swallow saliva.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a highly anxious patient reporting a persistent, worsening sensation of a midline throat mass causing acute fear of choking. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandatory to definitively evaluate airway patency, exclude acute epiglottitis or anaphylaxis via direct visualization, and immediately de-escalate the psychological crisis before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "There is a tight lump right in the middle of my throat, it feels like I am being choked and I keep trying to swallow it away but it won't budge."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a 48-hour exacerbation of a subjective sensation of a lump or tightness in the midline of the throat, between the suprasternal notch and thyroid cartilage. The sensation initially presented 3 weeks ago during a period of severe occupational stress. The tightness worsens significantly when swallowing dry saliva between meals. However, the patient notes complete relief of the sensation when actively eating solid food or drinking a large glass of water. There is a complete lack of progressive unintentional weight loss.
Functional Status: Independent but acutely fixated on the physical sensation, pacing the room.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound health anxiety regarding malignancy; patient lives alone.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/88 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Visibly anxious, constantly performing dry swallows, clear voice without stridor.
Physical Exam: Airway is entirely patent and secure. Oropharyngeal examination reveals completely normal, pink mucosa without erythema, masses, or exudates. Neck palpation reveals a normal, non-enlarged thyroid gland and a complete absence of a palpable neck mass or localized cervical lymphadenopathy. Vocal cord mobility is assumed intact based on completely unaffected voice quality. Patient observed drinking 100ml of water seamlessly without coughing or regurgitation. Documented history of a completely normal nasendoscopy 6 months prior for a similar episode.
Environmental Observations: Tidy environment; multiple empty water glasses on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Globus Sensation (Globus Pharyngeus) - Status: Acute hypertonic flare
Differentials:

True Food Bolus Impaction - Ruled out explicitly by the patient's successful and symptom-relieving ingestion of solid food and water.

Oesophageal Malignancy - Ruled out by the absence of progressive weight loss, complete lack of true dysphagia, and previously normal structural imaging.

Acute Epiglottitis - Ruled out due to completely normal voice, ability to swallow saliva, and complete absence of fever or tripod posturing.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours home visit due to severe, escalating panic regarding a perceived impending airway obstruction. The intensity of the physical sensation mimicking a true mechanical blockage mandated an immediate hands-on clinical examination of the airway and neck. By visually confirming airway patency, excluding a palpable cervical mass, and confirming the ability to swallow fluids without difficulty, the physician definitively diagnosed a benign functional spasm. This rapid, definitive exclusion of mechanical obstruction safely aborted a highly distressing psychological crisis and prevented an inappropriate emergency department presentation, strictly satisfying the criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered firm clinical reassurance regarding the benign nature of the muscular tension. Advised complete cessation of repetitive dry swallowing.
Positive Behavioral Change: When the sensation of the lump occurs, actively relax the shoulders and sip warm, decaffeinated tea rather than performing repeated, forceful dry swallows. The warm fluid will physically relax the cricopharyngeus muscle and break the spasm cycle, directly rewarding the patient with an immediate reduction in the choking sensation and reinforcing relaxation over panic.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to speech pathology for laryngeal massage and relaxation techniques.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 2 weeks to assess ongoing anxiety and stress management.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing an absolute inability to swallow liquids, sudden coughing up of blood, change in voice quality, or any objective difficulty drawing breath.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis relies on the absence of progressive mechanical symptoms; development of true pain when swallowing requires prompt re-evaluation.
Clinical Course: Globus is a muscle tension disorder of the throat, often driven by stress or reflux. It is not a tumor. The sensation will wax and wane with anxiety levels but poses zero risk to your airway.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you genuinely cannot swallow water, if your voice becomes permanently hoarse, or if you physically struggle to pull air into your lungs.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The sphincter muscle at the top of your swallowing tube has become hypertonic (too tight) due to stress. Because it is tight, you feel a 'lump'. Repeatedly dry swallowing forces that tight muscle to work without lubrication, making the spasm and the sensation worse.
Actionable Steps: Keep a bottle of water nearby. Whenever you feel the urge to forcefully clear your throat or dry swallow to check if the lump is there, take a slow sip of water instead. The water provides normal resistance, temporarily stretching and relaxing the muscle.

SMS INFORMATION

Diagnosis: Globus Sensation (throat muscle tension). Airway is completely safe, no mass found. Stop dry swallowing. Sip warm fluids. See your regular GP in 2 weeks.

SMS EDUCATION

Sip warm water instead of forcefully dry swallowing. Practice shoulder relaxation. Call 000 immediately if you genuinely cannot swallow liquids or cannot breathe.
